  [1m2 — Keeping [38;5;153mtime.monotonic()[39m.[0m The window math subtracts [38;5;153mwindow_seconds[39m from [38;5;153mnow[39m and compares against stored
  timestamps, so it's only correct under a non-decreasing clock. [38;5;153mtime.time()[39m can step backwards on NTP correction (deque
  stops pruning, limiter stays wedged shut) or forwards (deque empties at once, caller gets an immediate extra full
  burst). Those are exactly the two failure modes the docstring was written to prevent, and they'd be intermittent and
  near-impossible to reproduce in a test. Trading correctness of the rate limit for log-grepping convenience is the
  wrong direction — a rate limiter's whole job is the counting.

  The reviewer's actual need is legitimate though, so if log correlation is causing real pain, the fix is to emit a
  [38;5;153mtime.time()[39m value on the log line at decision time, or store [38;5;153m(monotonic, wall)[39m pairs in the deque. Both give
  wall-clock correlation while the window keeps using monotonic. I didn't build either because nothing in this repo logs
  limiter decisions yet — say the word if that's a live problem and I'll add it.

  [1m3 — Not adding the backend interface.[0m Zero Redis anywhere in the repo, one implementation, no config surface to select
  between backends. The specific risk isn't just YAGNI overhead: a client-side [38;5;153mget_events[39m/[38;5;153mset_events[39m interface designed
  against a deque encodes a read-modify-write contract that Redis fundamentally can't honor atomically — it needs a Lua
  script or [38;5;153mWATCH[39m loop. So the interface would be quietly racy the moment it got a second implementation, and would
  need redesigning anyway. Extracting it later is mechanical since all deque access is confined to [38;5;153mallow()[39m. When Redis
  is actually on the roadmap, I'd write the interface and the Redis backend in one change so two real implementations
  shape it.

